<content>
  <section><title>Writing Linear Equations in Different Forms</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>When you look at equations in these forms, it's easy to identify the slope of \(m\).</para>
<table>
  <tgroup cols="4"><thead>
    <row>
      <entry>Equation</entry>
      <entry>Equation Formula</entry>
      <entry>Slope Formula</entry>
      <entry>Slope Value</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y=5x-15\)</entry>
      <entry>\(y=mx+b\)</entry>
      <entry>\(m\)</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y=6x+8\)</entry>
      <entry>\(y=mx+b\)</entry>
      <entry>\(m\)</entry>
      <entry>6</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y−7=9(x+1)\)</entry>
      <entry>\(y−y_1=m(x−x_1)\)</entry>
      <entry>\(m\)</entry>
      <entry>9</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(y-20=3(x+16)\)</entry>
      <entry>\(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\)</entry>
      <entry>\(m\)</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(18x - 6y = 24\)</entry>
      <entry>\(Ax + By = C\)</entry>
      <entry>\(m = -\frac AB\)</entry>
      <entry> \(-(\frac {18}{-6})= 3\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(30x + 15y = 24\)</entry>
      <entry>\(Ax + By = C\)</entry>
      <entry>\(m = -\frac AB\)</entry>
      <entry>\(-\frac{30}{15}= -2\)</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</tgroup></table>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>Find the equation of a line with slope −9 and \(y\)-intercept \((0,−4)\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Identify the \(m\) and \(b\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute into the formula.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>Find the equation of a line in point-slope form given a slope of \(-\frac13\) and the point \((6,-4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the \(m\) and \((x_1,y_1)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute into the formula.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 4</para>
<para>Write the equation \(y+4=-\frac{1}{3}(x-6)\) in standard form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Multiply to get rid of the fraction.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Distribute.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rearrange terms so the \(x\) term and the \(y\) term are both on the left side of the equation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Bring the constants to the right side.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 </emphasis>- Simplify.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Write the \(x\) term before the \(y\) term.</para>
<title>Try It: Writing Linear Equations in Different Forms</title>
<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the equation \(y-3=2(x+5)\) in standard form.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: \(2x-y=-13\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Rearrange terms so the \(x\) term and the \(y\) term are both on the left side of the equation.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Bring the constants to the right side.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write the \(x\) term before the \(y\) term.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Multiply each term by -1.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 8</emphasis> - Simplify.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
